#{[17592186061861 :answer-field/correct #edb/ref 17592186061866] [17592186064634 :question/problem "Calculate\n\n$$\n\\int x2^{x}dx\n$$."] [17592186064634 :question/worked-solution "Use integration by parts with $u=x$ and $\\mathrm dv=2^x\\,\\mathrm dx$. Then $\\mathrm du=\\mathrm dx$ and $v=2^x/\\ln2$, so\n$$\n\\int x2^x\\,\\mathrm dx=\\frac{x2^x}{\\ln2}-\\frac1{\\ln2}\\int 2^x\\,\\mathrm dx\n=\\frac{2^x}{\\ln2}\\left(x-\\frac1{\\ln2}\\right)+C.\n$$\nDifferentiating the result gives $x2^x$, which verifies the antiderivative."] [17592186065444 :tutorial/content "In this lesson, we introduce a method of integration that can be used in cases where integration by substitution fails. The method is known as **integration by parts**. The so-called **by-parts formula** is \n\n$$\n\\int u\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}v}{\\textrm{d}x}\\,\\textrm{d}x= uv- \\int v\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\textrm{d}x}\\,\\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nor simply\n\n$$\n\\int uv'\\,\\textrm{d}x= uv- \\int vu'\\,\\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis method is handy for integrating products of functions where one factor can be easily integrated, and the other factor can easily be differentiated. \n\nLet's see how to apply it to evaluate the integral \n\n$$\n\\int xe^{2x} \\textrm{d}x\\,.\n$$\n\nThe idea is to call one of the factors $u,$ the other factor $v',$ and then apply the by-parts formula. We'll choose the following:\n\n$$\n\\int \\underbrace{\\color{blue}x}_{\\large\\color{blue}u}\\cdot \\underbrace{\\color{red}e^{2x}}_{\\large\\color{red}v'} \\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nNow, if ${\\color{blue}u} = {\\color{blue}x}$ then we differentiate to get ${\\color{blue}u'} = {\\color{blue}1}.$ And if ${\\color{red}v'} = {\\color{red}e^{2x}}$ then we integrate to get ${\\color{red}v} = {\\color{red}\\dfrac{1}{2}e^{2x}}.$ Note that we don't bother with the constant of integration here.\n\nWe now have everything we need, so we can substitute into the \"by-parts\" formula, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\underbrace{{\\color{blue}x}\\cdot {\\color{red}{e}^{2x}}}_{{\\color{blue}u}{\\color{red}{v}^{'}}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}x & =\\underbrace{{\\color{blue}x}\\cdot {\\color{red}\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2x}}}_{{\\color{blue}u}{\\color{red}v}}-\\int \\underbrace{{\\color{red}\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2x}}\\cdot {\\color{blue}1}}_{{\\color{red}v}{\\color{blue}{u}^{'}}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int x{e}^{2x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{2}x{e}^{2x}-\\frac{1}{2}\\int {e}^{2x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}x{e}^{2x}-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2x}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}x{e}^{2x}-\\frac{1}{4}{e}^{2x}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{e}^{2x}(2x-1)+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAnd we're done! Notice that integration by parts allows us to take a complicated integral $$\n\\displaystyle \\int xe^{2x}\\,\\textrm{d}x\n$$ and replace it with the simpler $$\n\\displaystyle \\int e^{2x}\\,\\textrm{d}x\n$$ plus some additional terms."] [17592186085088 :answer-field/correct #edb/ref 17592186085093] [17592186085468 :answer-field/correct #edb/ref 17592186085469] [17592186085473 :answer-field/correct #edb/ref 17592186085474] [17592186085478 :answer-field/correct #edb/ref 17592186085480] [17592186085483 :answer-field/correct #edb/ref 17592186085485] [17592186085489 :answer-field/correct #edb/ref 17592186085493] [17592186085494 :answer-field/correct #edb/ref 17592186085496] [17592186085499 :answer-field/correct #edb/ref 17592186085501] [17592186085504 :answer-field/correct #edb/ref 17592186085506] [17592186087269 :answer-field/correct #edb/ref 17592186087273] [17592186088032 :question/answer-fields #edb/ref 17592186401433] [17592186088059 :question/worked-solution "The equation is in standard form with $P(x)=1$ and $Q(x)=\\cos x$. An integrating factor is\n$$\nI(x)=e^{\\int 1\\,\\mathrm{d}x}=e^x.\n$$\nMultiplying through by $e^x$ and using the product rule gives\n$$\ne^xy'+e^xy=e^x\\cos x,\\qquad \\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}x}(ye^x)=e^x\\cos x.\n$$"] [17592186088140 :question/problem "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{2}{x}y=3,\\hspace{2em}x>0.\n$$\n\nSelect the correct options below:\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ An integrating factor for this equation is $I(x)=$ {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ The general solution is given by $y(x)=$ {{field-2}}."] [17592186088140 :question/worked-solution "For $x>0$, an integrating factor is\n$$\nI(x)=e^{\\int(2/x)\\,\\mathrm{d}x}=e^{2\\ln x}=x^2.\n$$\nMultiplying by $x^2$ and integrating gives\n$$\n(x^2y)'=3x^2,\\qquad x^2y=x^3+C,\\qquad y=x+\\frac{C}{x^2}.\n$$\nDifferentiation gives $y'+2y/x=3$, as required."] [17592186088141 :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem.\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+y=1,\\hspace{2em}y(0)=-3\n$$\n\nSelect the correct options below.\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ An integrating factor for this equation is $I(x)=$ {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ The solution to the initial value problem is $y(x)=$ {{field-2}}."] [17592186088141 :question/worked-solution "An integrating factor is $I(x)=e^x$. Thus\n$$\n(e^xy)'=e^x,\\qquad e^xy=e^x+C,\\qquad y=1+Ce^{-x}.\n$$\nThe initial condition gives $-3=1+C$, so $C=-4$. Therefore\n$$\ny(x)=1-4e^{-x}=1-\\frac4{e^x}.\n$$\nThis satisfies both $y'+y=1$ and $y(0)=-3$."] [17592186088142 :question/problem "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n2\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+y=3x.\n$$\n\nSelect the correct options below.\n$\\hspace{1em}$ An integrating factor for this equation is $I(x)=$ {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ The general solution is given by $y(x)=$ {{field-2}}."] [17592186088142 :question/worked-solution "First divide by $2$:\n$$\ny'+\\frac12y=\\frac32x.\n$$\nAn integrating factor is $I(x)=e^{x/2}$. Integration by parts gives\n$$\n(ye^{x/2})'=\\frac32xe^{x/2},\\qquad ye^{x/2}=3(x-2)e^{x/2}+C.\n$$\nHence\n$$\ny=3(x-2)+Ce^{-x/2}.\n$$\nSubstitution verifies $2y'+y=3x$."] [17592186088143 :question/problem "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-2y={x}^{2}{e}^{2x}.\n$$\n\nSelect the correct options below.\n$\\hspace{1em}$ An integrating factor for this equation is $I(x)=$ {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ The general solution is given by $y(x)=$ {{field-2}}."] [17592186088143 :question/worked-solution "An integrating factor is $I(x)=e^{-2x}$. Multiplying and integrating gives\n$$\n(ye^{-2x})'=x^2,\\qquad ye^{-2x}=\\frac{x^3}{3}+C.\n$$\nTherefore\n$$\ny=\\frac{x^3e^{2x}}3+Ce^{2x}.\n$$\nDifferentiating verifies $y'-2y=x^2e^{2x}$."] [17592186088436 :question/worked-solution "Here $P(x)=2x$, so an integrating factor is\n$$\nI(x)=e^{\\int 2x\\,\\mathrm{d}x}=e^{x^2}.\n$$\nMultiplication by $I(x)$ gives the exact derivative\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}x}(ye^{x^2})=e^{x^2}\\cdot 2xe^{-x^2}.\n$$\nThe product rule verifies the left side is $e^{x^2}(y'+2xy)$."]}
